# Root .gitattributes for the FLASHIda parent repo.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# -------------------
# The repo is checked out on Windows with `core.autocrlf=true` (system gitconfig,
# not repo-local), and until this file there was no root .gitattributes at all.
# So every text file git checks out here gets CRLF in the working tree -- proven
# today by `.claude/hooks/archive-implemented-specs.sh` (45 CRs) and by
# `.github/workflows/flashida-ci.yml` (608 CRs).
#
# The shell scripts, awk programs and container support files added for the local
# Docker CI are executed INSIDE Linux containers (and, for the hooks, under Git
# Bash). A CRLF checkout of any of them fails at the shebang with
#   /usr/bin/env: 'bash\r': No such file or directory
# or mid-script with `$'\r': command not found`. Forcing eol=lf here makes that
# impossible regardless of the developer's autocrlf setting.
#
# SCOPING -- READ BEFORE ADDING A RULE
# -----------------------------------
# There is deliberately NO `* text=auto` line. A repo-wide normalization rule
# would restate the eol policy for all 106 tracked files at once and is the one
# change here that could show the whole tree as modified. Every rule below is
# scoped to a filename pattern that either (a) matches only files created by the
# container work, or (b) matches an existing file whose index blob is ALREADY LF,
# so the clean filter turns the worktree CRLF back into the identical blob and
# `git status` stays clean. Case (b) is exactly the six `.claude/hooks/*.sh`
# scripts; verified with `git ls-files --eol` (i/lf for all six).
#
# These rules do NOT reach into the submodules. `FlashIDA/` and `OpenMS/` are
# separate repositories with their own .gitattributes -- FlashIDA's is
# `* text eol=crlf` and OpenMS's is `* text=auto eol=lf` -- and a parent-repo
# attributes file has no authority over either. In particular this file does not
# and must not change what `FlashIDA/test-data/**` looks like on disk; the design
# spec's CRLF-hazard note is explicit that normalizing those fixtures to LF would
# change what CI and the Windows container see.

# --- Shell, awk and container support files: LF everywhere, always -------------
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.bash          text eol=lf
*.awk           text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
Dockerfile.*    text eol=lf
*.Dockerfile    text eol=lf
.dockerignore   text eol=lf

# The `ci` dispatcher at the repo root is an extensionless bash script, so no
# pattern above reaches it. Anchored with a leading slash: this matches the root
# file named `ci` and nothing else. `ci.ps1` is listed too -- it is a thin shim
# that Git Bash / pwsh both read happily as LF, and keeping the pair identical
# avoids a mixed-ending pair of sibling files.
/ci             text eol=lf
/ci.ps1         text eol=lf

# Everything under docker/ is container payload. `text=auto` (not bare `text`)
# so that a binary asset dropped in there later is detected and left alone
# instead of being corrupted by eol conversion.
docker/**       text=auto eol=lf

# This file and .gitignore-style control files are read by tools that tolerate
# either ending; pinning the new one keeps it consistent with the scripts it
# governs. `.gitignore` is deliberately NOT listed -- it is an existing tracked
# file and leaving it unattributed keeps its on-disk form unchanged.
.gitattributes  text eol=lf
